 succeeded in 0ms:
  - two vowels in a row get a glide between them, y after i or e and w otherwise;
  - a consonant that can't stand where it is gets an added **u**;
  - the result has at least two syllables, counting a glide (**tuwu**).
- <span id="r-11-3"></span>The language's own vocabulary borrows only where the world shares a form, taken in that international form: **dolalu** dollar, **kilometulu**, **intelunetu**, **imeyilu**, **kamela**. A loan enters the vocabulary once it is common, and until then near-collisions are tolerated. Speakers also borrow freely from their own languages, as speakers of every language do. A borrowed word takes no **en** and is used like any word. Say it in its own sounds or the language's, and write it by its sound in the language's letters, as in the adapted form (11.2): **to nawi susi sa** "I ate two sushi". Casual writing often keeps the source spelling at first (*sushi*); borrowings settle into the language's letters as they're used. A borrowed word can do anything a word can, including be a verb (**to sin gugalu bu** "I googled it"), while a name with **en** is never a verb (**to sin ditun bu mon en google** "I searched for it on Google"). Put **en** before a borrowed word only if it could be taken for a word of the language. Borrowings that catch on drift toward the language's sounds; `tools/names.py` gives the written form. Native words, built words and phrases can always say the same thing.
- <span id="r-11-4"></span>Computers and interfaces use everyday words: search **ditun**, delete **nuton**, share **peton**, copy **gayen**, add **ginni**, download **dunu**, install **yaga**, turn on/off **benbi**/**tapi**, settings **ge finsa la**, computer **du sunbu** (`compounds.tsv`). **doku** "work" covers a machine working.
- <span id="r-11-5"></span>Letters used as labels (apartment 3B) pass through and are read as the speaker's culture reads them.
- To spell aloud for someone of another culture (a name, an email address, a code), say each letter by its word from the international spelling alphabet (ICAO), adapted to this language's sounds: a **alufa**, b **bulabo**, c **saluli**, d **deluta**, e **eko**, f **fokusutulotu**, g **golufu**, h **otelu**, i **indiya**, j **duliyetu**, k **kilo**, l **lima**, m **mayiku**, n **nobenbalu**, o **osuka**, p **papa**, q **kebeku**, r **lomeyo**, s **siyela**, t **tango**, u **yunifolumu**, v **bikuta**, w **wisuki**, x **ekusuleyi**, y **yanki**, z **sulu**. Within one culture its own letter names are fine. **kilo** is also "kilogram" (9.3.5); in spelling it is always a letter, as in the original alphabet.
- Read aloud, an address uses **mon** for @, **mimi** for the dot and **komu** for "com": *mara@gmail.com* is **mara mon gmail mimi komu**. A hashtag is **asutagu**. Other signs are said with ordinary words: * **uwan** "star", & **yen** "and".
- <span id="r-11-6"></span>New words are made by the method in [vocabulary-method.md](vocabulary-method.md).

## 12. Conditions and modality

- <span id="r-12-1"></span>A condition is **don** "if" + clause. **kon** + number after **don** says how likely the condition is: **don kon fi** "if (unlikely)". **don kon po** "if (and it isn't so)" is counterfactual: **don kon po to sin isi, to sin nagu wan** "if I had gone, I would have seen her". The main clause is the consequence, and there is no "would".
- <span id="r-12-2"></span>There are three kinds of modality: ability, with **lenu** "can"; obligation, with **keya** "must" and **wupi** "need to" ("should" is advice, the act **fon**, 7.1); and permission, with **efon** "allow" (**me gun efon nawi** "you may eat"). There, **gun** + an action word takes a verb as its object, as the plain verb would (4.2.6): "you are allowed to eat". Probability is confidence (6.1).
- <span id="r-12-3"></span>Purpose is **li** + verb, "in order to": **li bika** "to buy".
- <span id="r-12-4"></span>"Let's" is **so pen** …, and "don't" is **tin mu** ….

## 13. Grammar words

<span id="r-13-1"></span>

| Group | Words |
|---|---|
| Speech acts | question **min** · request **so** · instruct **tin** · advise **fon** · intend **de** · warn **yo** · exclaim **ba** |
| Politeness | **nun** |
| Clause operator | confidence **kon** |
| Phrase operators | not **mu** · only **na** · also **fe** · even **wi** · focus **o** |
| Degree | more **ke** · very **bi** |
| Aspect | done **sin** · ongoing **le** |
| Sentence end | tag **min** · "you know" **a** |
| Pronouns | I **to** · you **me** · we incl. **pen** · we excl. **din** · he/she **wan** · the other one **yi** · it **bu** · logophoric **sun** · anyone **go** · self **nen** |
| Determiners | plural **la** · this **tan** · that **ben** · all **we** · some **in** · some-not-all **kin** · none **fa** · unspecified **bo** · which/what **yu** |
| Relations | of **pi** · at **mon** · to **tun** · from/than **se** · with **gan** · for **li** · before **ya** · after **wo** · like/about **fu** · during **nin** · because of **gi** |
| Linkers | and **yen** · or **lu** · but **un** · if **don** · because **gi** · that **wun** · who/which **man** · while **nin** · before **ya** · after **wo** |
| Copula | is **gun** |
| Names | **en** |
| Derivation | doer **no** · thing **ge** · tool **du** · place **pu** |

<span id="r-13-2"></span>These operators have two syllables, stressed on the first: not literal **genso**, seen **wanme**, told **inwun**, inferred **wemen**, at least **konsin**, about to **buntu**, rather **tason**, almost **nedin**, each **mesun**, usually **manni**, "okay?" **buni**, "it turned out" **donon**, title **lanno**, until **anko**, without **nuwen**, end of phrase **kiyo**, number label **mena**. **mena** goes between a noun and a number to make a label or an ordinal (5.2). **anko** and **nuwen** are relation words, used like **ya** and **gan**: **to doku anko kegin** "I work until evening", **wan obu nuwen nimo** "he came without money". "Against" in the sense of protection is **se** "from" (**kuwi wan se ben** "protect him against that"); in the sense of opposing it is the verb **ninsu** "oppose".

## 14. Vocabulary

- <span id="r-14-1"></span>[../data/lexicon.tsv](../data/lexicon.tsv) lists roots and loans, each with a field and a frequency tier (1 rare – 5 most common). [../data/grammar.tsv](../data/grammar.tsv) lists the grammar words, [../data/numbers.tsv](../data/numbers.tsv) the number words and [../data/compounds.tsv](../data/compounds.tsv) the established compounds.
- <span id="r-14-2"></span>A gloss with a second gloss after a comma fixes the sense: **gufu** "hard, not soft". A root means that sense only.
- <span id="r-14-3"></span>One root may cover senses that most language families lump together: **muge** "do; make". Context tells them apart.

